      <workList xml:id="workDesc_8fbe2573">
         <work xml:id="work_id8cb8c74c">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_201598ab">C1:007</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_id9228aba0" xml:lang="de">Compendium musices theoretico-practicum</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_8321dd9a"/>
            <creation xml:id="creation_id2e214ee1">
               <date isodate="1735" xml:id="date_795cfb6f">1735</date>
               <geogName xml:id="geogName_id466ade81">Leipzig</geogName>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_id6fcfc07d">
               <p xml:id="idm145">The first music theoretical treatise which Scheibe wrote was his <rend xml:id="idm146" fontstyle="italic">Compendium musices</rend>. It is possible that Scheibe’s original manuscript was acquired by his student <persName xml:id="idm147" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Niels Schiørring</persName> sometime after 1776, for Schiørring's catalogue from around 1780 including music theoretical books and manuscripts also lists ‘Scheibe, <rend xml:id="idm148" rend="underline">Compendium musices</rend>, 1735 (4<rend xml:id="idm149" rend="sup">o</rend>)’. Unfortunately the manuscript seems to have been lost. The dating is important for the extant transcript in Leipzig does not contain a date though Benary (1957) believes it to have been made around 1730. Since Scheibe refers to a couple of printed musical treatises (<persName xml:id="idm150" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Mattheson</persName>’s <rend xml:id="idm151" fontstyle="italic">Critica musica</rend> (1722–25), and also <persName xml:id="idm152" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann David Heinichen</persName>’s <rend xml:id="idm153" fontstyle="italic">Generalbaß</rend> of 1728), it is possible to determine the earliest probable date to be around 1728. Another transcript, which apparently <persName xml:id="idm154" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Christoph Graupner</persName> owned, carries the year 1736. Thus Scheibe most likely wrote the treatise during his student years in Leipzig, before he left on his tour in Easter 1735 which ended when he arrived in Hamburg the following year. The surviving presentation copy in Leipzig was produced by a professional copyist, presumably because Scheibe had a terrible handwriting that was difficult to read – as he himself remarks later in life. It seems plausible that originally Scheibe intended the work for publication and might have sent it to Breitkopf. Yet it cannot have been a printer’s manuscript as Scheibe mentions in the introduction that 'incidentally nothing will be printed of the mathematical parts, in so far as they deal with harmony' (fol. 3r). The treatise was never published; later it was acquired by the music collector in Leipzig <persName xml:id="idm155" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Carl Ferdinand Becker</persName>, perhaps from Breitkopf. The subjects of the treatise are: sound, intervals, harmony, cadences, counterpoint (e.g. imitation, fugue, canon), melody and even the church modes and the different musical styles ('Kirchen-, Theatral- and Cammer-Styl') and genres. It should be noted that these subjects also appear in his final publication, <rend xml:id="idm156" fontstyle="italic">Über die musikalische Composition</rend> (1773). The three distinct musical styles became a subject of discussion, not only in <rend xml:id="idm157" fontstyle="italic">Der Critische Musicus</rend> (1738–40), but also in the lesser known tract, <rend xml:id="idm158" fontstyle="italic">Gültige Zeugnisse</rend> of 1738 (SchW C1:009) which was a response to Mattheson’s <rend xml:id="idm159" fontstyle="italic">Kern melodischer Wißenschaft</rend> (1737). Also Mattheson dealt with the three styles.</p>
            </history>
